.TH SAM 1
.SH NAME
sam \- screen editor with structural regular expressions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wm/sam
[
.B -d
]
[
.I file ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sam
is Plan 9's multi-file editor, running entirely inside Inferno.
As on Plan 9 it is two programs joined by a byte pipe speaking the sam
terminal protocol: the terminal
.RB ( /dis/wm/sam.dis )
draws the windows and handles the mouse and keyboard, and the host
.RB ( /dis/wm/samengine.dis )
holds the files and runs the command language.
The terminal loads the host as a module and spawns it; no host
operating system binary is involved.
.PP
The terminal follows Plan 9's samterm
.RI ( flayer.c ,
.IR menu.c ,
.IR scroll.c
and the mouse half of
.IR main.c ).
Sam is a single window.
The command window runs across its top fifth; every window on a file is
a layer inside sam's window, and layers overlap.
The current layer, the one typing goes to, is in front with a heavy
border; the others have a thin one.
Button 1 in a layer that is not current makes it current; in the
current layer it selects.
Buttons 2 and 3 act on the current layer wherever they are pressed.
.PP
Each layer has a scroll bar at its left showing the part of the file in
the layer.
In the current layer's scroll bar, on release, button 1 moves the text
back as many lines as the pointer is below the top of the bar, button 3
brings the line beside the pointer to the top, and button 2 moves to
the place in the file corresponding to the pointer's height.
.PP
When a new layer is needed the cursor becomes a box and a rectangle is
swept out with button 3.
A click instead of a sweep takes the whole window or, while the command
window has one layer, the part of it on the clicked side of the command
window.
Another button abandons the sweep, and a rectangle no bigger than 100
by 40 pixels makes no layer.
When sam's window is resized every layer is scaled with it.
.PP
The files named on the command line are put in the file menu; the
first is the current file, but it is read, and a layer swept for it,
only when a command first uses it.
As in sam, after any command that makes another file current or reads
the current one, the terminal brings that file's layer to the front
(sweeping one if it has none); while the command window is current, the
file's layer only comes to the front, and typing stays in the command
window.
The command window, listed in the menu as
.BR ~~sam~~ ,
takes commands typed at its end; a command runs when its line is ended
with a newline.
Output and error messages (which begin with
.BR ? )
appear in the command window.
.PP
Button 2 in a file window offers
.BR cut ,
.BR paste ,
.BR snarf ,
.B look
(search for the literal selected text) and, once there is one,
.BI / regexp
(search again for the last regular expression).
In the command window the last item is
.BR send ,
which runs the selected text as a command.
Button 3 offers
.B new
(sweep a layer for an unnamed file),
.BR zerox ,
.BR resize ,
.B close
and
.BR write ,
followed by the file names.
For
.BR zerox ,
.BR resize ,
.B close
and
.B write
the cursor becomes a bullseye and the layer to act on is chosen with
button 3;
.B zerox
then sweeps another layer on the same file (at most five per file),
.B resize
sweeps a new place for the layer, and
.B close
closes the layer, or the file if it is its last.
While the host is busy the commands are shown in parentheses and
ignored.
As in sam, each file name is preceded by
.B \'
if the file is modified, by
.BR - ,
.B +
or
.B *
for no, one or several windows, and by
.B .
if it is the current file.
Choosing a file with no window sweeps one; choosing one with windows
brings its front layer up, or the next if that one is current.
Each menu appears with its last choice under the pointer.
A double click selects a word, a line, or the text between matching
brackets or quotes.
The snarf buffer is
.BR /chan/snarf ,
shared with other applications.
.PP
The
.B -d
option traces the terminal protocol to
.B samterm.log
in the current directory.
.SS Addresses
An address selects a range of text, as in Plan 9
.IR sam (1):
.TF "/regexp/, ?regexp?"
.TP
.B .
dot, the current selection
.TP
.I n
line
.I n
.TP
.BI # n
the empty string after character
.I n
.TP
.B $
the end of the file
.TP
.B '
the mark set by
.B k
.TP
.BI / regexp / ", ?" regexp ?
the next match forwards or backwards, wrapping around the file
.TP
.BI \(dq regexp \(dq
dot in the file whose menu line matches
.TP
.IB a1 + a2 ", " a1 - a2
.I a2
evaluated forwards or backwards from
.I a1
(a missing
.I a2
is 1, a line)
.TP
.IB a1 , a2 ", " a1 ; a2
from the start of
.I a1
to the end of
.IR a2 ;
with
.B ;
dot is set to
.I a1
before
.I a2
is evaluated.
A missing
.I a1
is 0 and a missing
.I a2
is
.BR $ .
.PD
.SS Regular expressions
Regular expressions are sam's: leftmost longest,
.B ^
and
.B $
match at the ends of every line,
.B \en
matches a newline and
.B .
never does.
An empty regular expression means the last one used.
.SS Commands
Commands apply to the current file; the default address of most is dot.
.TF "x/regexp/ command"
.TP
.BI a/ text /
Append text after the range.
.I Text
may instead follow on lines of its own, ended by a line holding only
.BR . .
.TP
.BI i/ text / ", c/" text /
Insert before, or change, the range.
.TP
.B d
Delete the range.
.TP
.BI s n / regexp / text /[g]
Substitute
.I text
for the
.IR n th
(default first) match in the range, or all matches with
.BR g .
In
.IR text ,
.B &
is the match and
.BI \e d
the
.IR d th
parenthesised subexpression.
.TP
.BI m " address" ", t" " address"
Move or copy the range after
.IR address .
.TP
.B p
Print the range and set dot to it.
.TP
.BR = " [" # ]
Print the line and character address of the range.
.TP
.B k
Set the mark to the range.
.TP
.BI x/ regexp / " command"
For each match in the range, set dot to it and run
.IR command .
With no regular expression, each line.
.TP
.BI y/ regexp / " command"
The same for the text between matches.
.TP
.BI g/ regexp / " command" ", v/" regexp / " command"
Run
.I command
if the range does (does not) contain a match.
.TP
.BI X/ regexp / " command" ", Y/" regexp / " command"
Run
.I command
in each file whose menu line does (does not) match.
.TP
.BI { " commands " }
Run each command, one per line, with dot set to the range.
.TP
.BI "u " n
Undo the last
.I n
(default 1) changes to the file;
.BI u- n
redoes them.
.TP
.BI "w " file
Write the range (default the whole file).
.TP
.BI "e " file ", r " file
Replace the file's text, or the range, with
.IR file 's
contents.
.TP
.BI "f " name
Set the file's name.
.TP
.BI "b " file ", B " "file ..."
Make a file current, or load files into the menu.
.TP
.BI "D " "file ..."
Remove files from the menu.
.TP
.B n
List the files, flagging
.B '
modified,
.B +
open in a window and
.B .
current.
.TP
.B q
Quit.
.TP
.BI ! cmd ", <" cmd ", >" cmd ", |" cmd
Run
.I cmd
with
.IR sh (1):
its output replaces the range
.RB ( < ),
the range is its input
.RB ( > ),
or both
.RB ( | ).
.TP
.BI "cd " dir
Change directory.
.PD
.PP
As in sam, all the changes a command makes are addressed in the text as
it was when the command began and are applied together when it ends,
so they must be in sequence;
.B u
undoes a whole command.
Closing a modified file, or quitting with modified files, warns once.
.SH FILES
.TF /chan/snarf
.TP
.B /chan/snarf
the shared snarf buffer
.PD
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/wm/sam.b
